  + `/watch` command handler and project-type detection for auto-watch; the post-prompt watch cycle is skipped entirely when the prompt changed zero files (`should_run_watch_after_prompt` gate). Day 175 (#818, found by blind round 71): that gate takes a **per-turn baseline** — `should_run_watch_after_prompt(changes, edits_before)` is `changes.edit_count() > edits_before`, and `run_watch_after_prompt` threads an `edits_before: usize` from each caller. It used to be `!changes.snapshot().is_empty()`, i.e. the **session-wide** deduped file list, which `SessionChanges::clear()` only empties on `/clear` — so once anything had been edited in the session it returned `true` forever and the predicate could not answer the per-turn question its own doc comment claims ("no write/edit tool calls **this turn**"). **The unit that matters here is call sites, not the function**, and that is why the defect survived: three of the four callers were safe for three *different* reasons — `repl.rs` gates the call on its own outer per-turn delta (`edit_count() > ctx.edits_before`, from #774), and both `main.rs` sites are per-process, where session-wide *is* per-turn (they pass a literal `0`, so their behaviour is byte-identical). The fourth, `conversations.rs`'s `/extended`, had **no** outer guard, so the broken predicate was the only gate: after any earlier edit in the session, a zero-change `/extended` turn ran the full lint→fix→test cycle and, on failure, `build_watch_fix_prompt` told the agent *"Your changes caused failures"* about a pre-existing failure the turn did not cause — precisely the misattribution the gate exists to prevent, costing a wasted watch run plus up to `MAX_WATCH_FIX_ATTEMPTS = 3` API fix turns aimed at a lie. That call site samples its baseline **before** the prompt runs (a baseline read afterwards would already contain the turn's own writes — the Day-165 shape, where a guard that reads the world after its own action is blinded by that action). The **counter**, not the vec length, is the right input: a turn that re-edits an already-touched file grows `edit_count()` while leaving `snapshot().len()` unchanged. **Why no test caught it:** both pre-existing gate tests drive a *fresh* `SessionChanges`, where session-wide and per-turn state are identical, so neither could distinguish the two readings — vacuous green in exactly the direction that mattered. Both are kept verbatim (baseline `0` preserves their exact meaning) and a third drives a **non-fresh** tracker, asserting the skip in the direction the old code got wrong *and* the near-miss that must still pass through (re-editing the same file is work this turn), with an explicit precondition that the deduped set did not grow. Verified to fail against the old body before landing, so it is a real regression guard rather than a fixture agreeing with the fix.
